ffice at the  Commission some 16 or 17 of the people  that I appointed to those offices were  either independence or Republicans and  the Clinton White House did not object  to a single one of them never sent me  any one that had been active in a  campaign to employ and greatly respected  the independence of the agency at a time  when our funding was jeopardized I went  to Larry Summers and told him that our  bill is combined with a lot of important  bills and I know the pressure on  President Clinton to sign that bill will  be enormous but I hope you can get him  to agree to veto the bill if the  viability of the SEC is placed in  question within several hours Larry came  back and said the president has agreed  to do it he was enormously supportive I  met with him alone just about the only  time that I did meet with him alone a  day or so before he left office and  after the usual kind of formalities I  said mr. president you know I wondered  why I haven't heard from you more or  seen more of you during eight years I've  been here we've certainly been involved  in a lot of issues some of them were  pretty tough for you in terms of  the politics of the issue and I just  wondered if you felt about me as you  might a postmaster in Little Rock any  smile then he said no he said with you  and Bob Rubin Larry Summers and some of  the others that I knew knew one another  in private life and were real  professionals I respected you and I  wanted to allow you to do your job and I  think that intelligence and that  sensitivity produced a darn good  economic team and the fact that Reuben  and I and Greenspan had known one  another for many years really helped  policy-making when you get to Washington  your staff is inclined to tell you well  you can't trust Treasury or you can't  trust the controllers office they'll eat  your lunch and they'll play on the turf  battles well they couldn't do that with  us because we knew one another too well  and I think that element of trust 