w products are  available today yes what are your  thoughts on the relationship between the  Bush administration and the SEC the  relationship between the Bush  administration and the SEC I think the  president has gone along with a  substantial increase in the budget of  the SEC he has spoken about the  importance of stamping out fraud in our  markets has put the prestige of the  administration behind the Commission and  I think you can ask for no more from an  administration and I applaud the  initiatives that he has embraced  yes my ibanking colleagues are gonna  dislike this question flawed in the  sense that prices are set deliberately  low money's left on the table from a for  the company that's going public and  retail investors are generally shut out  what is your opinion on that and do you  think government and the SEC  specifically has a role in helping to  change that and make it more efficient  I'm often asked this question about the  fact that I POS initial public offerings  wind up in the hands of institutions and  very little of it goes to retail  investors well I don't think the process  of allocating I POS the basic process of  allocating it to your best customer is  wrong I think that's a standard and a  reasonable business practice I believe  that if there is if there are payments  involve for those allocations if  investors are expected to buy in the  aftermarket or buy at higher prices  that's a manipulative practice and  that's a practice that is a violation of  the securities laws and those instances  have been under investigation and I  think you will see cases brought by the  Commission as they should be I think  that the practice of using research to  hype companies that really are  undeserving of that is wrong and I think  American investors have to be far more  questioning about buying IPOs I think  anyone who makes the purchase of IPOs  the basis of their investing strategy  deserves to lose their money it's a dumb  strategy most IPOs don't work out  and it's the greed factor at work and I  don't have much sympathy for investors  who play that game and then complain  because they find that they've lost  money but to the extent to which there  have been manipulations that have taken  place that is that is totally wrong  hi exactly a question that I have is you  mentioned several times about investor  education and I know your book is one of  the ways to educate and University of  Michigan could you be a little bit more  specific as to what other area that we  could become a better private investor  well I can't think of a better area than  investor education I think that with  respect to 401ks that's the greatest  universe of uneducated investors in the  world the Labor Department simply lacks  the resources to do what they should do  to teach investors on which options  should be pursued and how to understand  when a proxy is really reveals a  terrible flaw in a company and I think  that process of education is not  something that can necessarily be taught  in high schools or universities or  graduate schools it's pretty pretty  obvious pretty plain and i think that  the SEC has begun that process and  should continue it I think the town  meetings allowing investors to ask  questions is a pretty good way of doing  it I think that investor protection in  this country is a function of kind of a  trilogy of three things the enforcement  efforts of the SEC to bring actions  against violations of our security laws  they can't assure investors against all  fraud but they can see the most common  cases internet fraud for instance and  they can come down hard on those cases  making them a symbol for what investors  should avoid secondly  the work of the self-regulating  organizations the work of the New York  Stock Exchange and the NASD whose  enforcement arm is has become  increasingly sophisticated to protect  investors the NASD is about to bring a  major case involving research on its  misuse and finally private rights of  action the rights of individuals to sue  under our securities laws when they have  been defrauded and those three elements  private rights of action SROs and the  enforcement efforts of the SEC in my  judgment give us the best protected  universe of investors and the best  protected markets in the history of the  world yes  I have any of the problems you've talked  about tonight been increased or enabled  by the Securities Reform Act of nineteen  ninety five and have any of the  provisions of that been repealed the  Securities Reform Act that of 1995 that  I was just asked about the question was  whether any of the problems resulted  from that act which to some extent made  it somewhat more difficult to bring  frivolous lawsuits I don't really  believe so I think there were some  aspects of that act that were productive  I think that the SEC tried to see to it  that investor interests were protected  and I think the greatest fears that we  had were not realized the act as it was  originally constructed called for losers  to pay for litigation and I oppose that  strenuously because I felt that would  simply say that people who didn't have  money simply couldn't afford to sue and  I think that's a an essential protection  and the Act didn't include that I do not  personally believe the litigation reform  act brought about what we have  experienced in recent months and years I  think what brought about what we've  experienced in recent months and years  has been the failure of all our  gatekeepers and what I referred you  before as the suspension of disbelief by  a public that was willing to go along  with anything by research analysts who  saw every recommendation go up by  investment bankers who saw every  underwriting succeed by brokers or so  recommendation go up in all of these  energies thought it was their genius  that produce these results rather than  the sweep of a bull market which carried  a lot of bad stuff along with a lot of  good  well you've been a great audience I  won't ask you any additional questions I  hope that some of you buy my book but  more importantly than that I hope you  kind of carry the message ask the  questions find out whether you're your  Congressman your senator cares read  proxy statements look for boards that  are corrupted we've got lots of them  because you know the American people are  a friendly people we're a collegial  people and when we're invited to go on  boards we tend to go if we know people  on those boards or ave serve on our  boards and how likely are we to raise  our hands and object well before Enron  Anderson not very likely today I think  audit committees and boards of directors  have been put on notice and I'm very  often challenged with well it's going to  be so hard to get directors today it  will and it should be because directors  have a great and almost sacred  responsibility and they're basically the  lifeline in terms of investor protection  that will do much more than any of the  rules or congressional fulminations or  anything we write about or speak about  and some of you serve on boards will  serve on boards no board members and  I hope you won't despair you'll ask the  tough questions and you'll keep coming  back thanks a lot  Arthur Levitt served as chairman of the  SEC during the Clinton administration  from nineteen ninety three to two  thousand his book is take on the street  what Wall Street in corporate America  don't want you to know what you can do  to fight back it's published by Pantheon  books an imprint of Random House visit  them online at Random House calm /  pantheon monday is President's Day and  you're watching a three-day weekend of  books on book

