ortuned by  companies to give them favorable ratings  and in return for which companies would  share with them information that was not  generally available to the investing  public so we embarked on a rulemaking  that resulted in regulation fair  disclosure reg FD as it came to be known  was bitterly opposed by the securities  industry the analysts community almost  universally opposed this regulation the  regulation called for any material  information  about a publicly owned company must be  released at the same time to the public  as well as to institutions as well as  the analyst community by and large  corporate America embraced this as  practices which they had adhered to  anyway and that was a terribly important  regulation now any of you who have been  part of government know that no matter  how well conceived a a rule or a  regulation may be almost always that  rule of regulation has unforeseen  consequences and it's the job of careful  regulators to measure that and make  mid-course adjustments another  rulemaking that we went through and  probably caused me the greatest  aggravation at any time that I was at  the Commission started about six months  after i came to the Commission if  someone had told me that most of my time  in Washington most of the eight years  would be spent dealing with accounting  issues I would have challenged them on  that I'm obviously not an accountant  never took an accounting course and have  the greatest admiration for those  hard-working people who are auditors but  I began to see more and more cases  coming through our enforcement division  of accounting fraud of numbers that were  manipulated and massaged this was not  just the accountants it was corporate  America that got caught up in the hype  and euphoria of a 15-year bull market  please forgive me these are that  virtually the first words I've spoken  I've just gotten rid of terrible case of  laryngitis I went to the firm's and I  tried to persuade them that it was  essential that they rid themselves of  the kinds of conflicts that were  creating these problems the economics of  the accounting industry changed  dramatically during the period of the  80s and 90s firms that had derived most  of their revenues from auditing began to  derive more and more revenues from  non-audit services consulting services  and companies very often publicly owned  companies were paying perhaps four and  five times as much for consulting  services as they did for auditing  services now I know many of you and  particularly those in the accounting  department know that in order is not  formula at a typical audit calls for  perhaps 75 to a hundred judgment calls  subjective judgments that could either  favor management or could favor  shareholders where is that call likely  to be made in the event of a huge  consulting contract as you walk through  the airport in Detroit in laguardia at  LAX in San Francisco and looked at the  signs put up by the accounting  profession during the 90s you'd have a  very difficult time in determining what  business these people were in they  certainly weren't auditors you didn't  see people in green eyeshades they  didn't talk about trust they didn't talk  about integrity they didn't talk about  accuracy they talked in a language that  was almost mystical  about services and concepts that had no  relevance to the investing public you  don't see those signs anymore because  the accounting industry was led by  individuals who by being such fierce  competitors between themselves lost  sight of the basic franchise given to  them by the government to place the  public interest above all else and where  did they go or for help when we were  negotiating a settlement with the  industry they went directly to Congress  and spent millions of dollars hiring  lobbyists that harassed the SEC  mercilessly and threatened the funding  of the agency during my years in  Washington I don't think we ever  initiated a single pro investor  initiative that was not opposed by  various members of the Congress and the  Senate and the reason for that was that  campaign contributions had become so  major a part of life in Washington that  the notion of getting the oversight  committees of a regulating agency to  harass that agency and tie them up in an  endless series of hearings and letters  that most rules most regulations never  see the light of day a regulator can be  most effective however by using the  bully pulpit available to him by going  directly to the people from my years  outside of Washington I knew that the  view of Washington by most Americans is  that this is a place where bureaucrats  operate in  protected offices throwing down  regulations that are costly like  Thunderbolts from atop Mount Olympus and  I started a program of traveling around  the country and conducting town meetings  where people came out to ask me  questions at first 40 people came to  hear me in Cherry Valley New Jersey and  then it grew 500 people in Houston a  thousand people in Columbus we had 43 of  these meetings in Los Angeles we  attracted 8,000 people for two hours  they asked questions how to choose a  mutual fund how to choose a broker why  didn't you answer my letter that I sent  you four months ago but they  participated in the process and at each  and every one of these meetings I  brought a member of the Commission's  oversight committee a typical  congressman cannot draw a thousand  people to a town meeting and they were  impressed with that and I think because  of that we were given more leeway than  we might otherwise have had and we were  probably the most proactive commission  that has been around for many many years  but getting back to the accountants I  met with the heads of the big five firms  at that time and asked them if they  would compromise the issue of separating  accounting auditing from consulting  services the then-head of Arthur  Andersen stood up at that meeting and  said if you go ahead with this Arthur  this will be war and war it was I think  they were foolish because we immediately  called for a series of public hearings  around America we're businesspeople  academics politicians consumerist all  got up and testify  to the fact that the accounting industry  had violated the contract that they had  with America they suffered a perceptual  blow of enormous magnitude I remember  going to my dentist in Washington around  that period and as I ease myself into  the chair of pain the dentist turned to  me and said you know Arthur those  accounts are sure a bunch of burglars  aren't they well when dentist start to  talk about accountants that way you know  you've got a problem well as we came to  the last part of that rule making  process we were threatened literally the  lifeline of the Commission was  threatened I've learned that in  Washington your friends may have very  different ideologies than you have and  indeed my closest relationships in  Washington my closest friend was with  senator phil gramm we agreed on nothing  absolutely nothing except we both loved  labrador retrievers and phil gramm was  the head of the powerful banking  committee when he heard that there was  an effort afoot to cut the funding of  the SEC if we proceeded with this  rulemaking he said to me Arthur I  disagree with you totally on this  proposal but I will fight to my last  breath to prevent legislative sleight of  hand from standing in your way senator  ted stevens of alaska another  conservative Republican and Judd Gregg  of New Hampshire all rose to the defense  of the Commission but if I had to name  individuals in Washington who really  cared about America's investors I could  probably not get  yes the fingers of my right hand  tomorrow morning on Capitol Hill there  will be lobbyists the New York Stock  Exchange lobbyists for the accounting  firms the law firms lobbyists for  corporate America but nobody will be  representing the interests of what is  potentially the most influential force  in the country and that's America's  investing public one of the reasons that  I wrote this book was to persuade  investors that their interests are very  much involved and that they simply have  to organize better than they have  through groups such as the consumers  Federation of America AARP the AF of  l-cio The Motley Fool and other groups  of that caliber and I think that is  beginning to take place in the absence  of that there are so many evidences of  Congress really neglecting investor  interests yes there are some members of  the House people like John Dingell who  represents this area Ed Markey and a su  from California and a number of others  who care about these issues but very few  members of Congress understand issues of  market structure or market regulation or  even investor protection and the  congressman who in recent months sound  like latter-day Elmer Gantry xand  calling for rules and regulations most  of which make no sense at all and are  overly costly they're the very ones who  tried to choke the SEC and they're the  ones as the passion for reform abates  will be back in their mode of doing  what's best for their contributors so it  was my hope that this  book in touching on the seven deadly  sins of mutual funds that not many fund  investors know about in talking about  alternatives you have to dealing with  brokers in talking about the plumbing in  terms of your options to choose a  different market on which to execute  your orders that investors might become  smarter investors I really believe that  a dollar spent on educating investors is  far more effective than a dollar spent  on regulation now all of this cascaded  once Enron came on the scene and  immediately the American public  galvanized the politicians to come up  with ideas and schemes and measures and  the finger of blame was pointed mostly  at the accountants also with the lawyers  but the blame doesn't just belong to the  accountants and the lawyers it's also  the standard setters the rating agencies  the regulators the boards of directors  and the public itself we all got caught  up in a period of hype euphoria where  disbelief was suspended and individuals  willing to pay a hundred times nothing  for companies that were worth just about  that were snapped up by a gullible  public and all of us had our share of  responsibility and now we're where we  are today we have an accounting  profession that has changed almost all  of its leadership they're beginning to  understand the relevance of the public  interest and I think that today's  leaders of the accounting  firms are trying to restore the  tarnished image of the profession  clearly the leaders of Corporate America  are aware of the essential importance of  the restoration of public confidence the  leaders of Corporate America during the  90s were the muscular leaders who would  buy a company with a stroke of a pen a  few months later fire 20,000 employees  and two or three years down the road  sell that company for a huge loss we  have in my judgment a different quality  of leadership in corporate America today  more sensitive more interested in public  private sector partnerships more  committed to restoring public confidence  we've been here before we were here  after teapot dome and after penn central  right now we're in a pretty good place  in my judgment in the economy and in our  markets and the public hasn't recognized  this yet they are frightened they are  disillusioned and by and large they  don't want to hear about the stock  market I think that I'm basically very  optimistic about our markets and where  we're going well I've covered a lot of  subjects I obviously care a lot about  these issues I'd like to stop now and  try to answer your questions which feel  free to ask me any easy question you can  about anything about my how I got my job  what my relationship with Bill Clinton  was what I think of how you what I think  of my success or just about anything you  want to ask I will try to answer for you  and I hope this motivates you to to buy  my book because if you don't I'll feel  rejected so I will try to answer those  questions yes dan why are with the mba  class of 03 I did not buy your book i  bought the cds okay and it's never too  late well well I bought him on ebay and  I plan to sell them again on ebay effect  but they were in their original wrapper  just wondering about your opinion about  the cut the question of deduct ability  of dividends and whether or not there  might be another way other than making  them tax-deductible to make them not  double taxed and what your view is of  that I think that the short term  implications in my judgment of the bill  as it's been presented or the outline of  it that the president gave will not be  of much consequence I think the  long-term implications I think will  motivate a change in market behavior it  will be very costly in my judgment but I  think it will have a number of  implications which I think will be  favorable I think companies will begin  to think about the desirability of  paying dividends I think it will move us  somewhat away from the short term  investment perspective that we have in  this country  I think that investors will begin to  look at dividends differently than they  have in the past if this is intended as  device to jumpstart the economy I don't  think it's going to work if it's  intended as a device to change market  behavior which in my judgment it clearly  is not I think it will have some impact  which will be favorable I believe that  the bill is not going to pass in its  present form but I think it will some  aspects of it will be will be retained  do you have any view of the effort by  mutual funds to almost self regulate  themselves these days and that  Commission the question was do I have  any feeling about before you answer  could we get people who have questions  please to come to the platform there's  two microphones set up between the two  tiers if you would please just step up  to the microphone be prepared to answer  a question the question I was asked was  about the proposal floated by the SEC  last week and discussed today by the  commission of setting up a separate  self-regulatory organization for the  mutual fund industry I'm not comfortable  with that proposal self-regulation works  up to a point it works effectively only  if there is oversight and during my  years at the Commission we brought cases  against the new york stock exchange the  American Stock Exchange the Philadelphia  the Boston the pacific chicago and the  chicago board options exchange just  about every exchange in america for not  pursuing the interests of investors now  by and large we couldn't do it without  having self regulation but I think with  the doubling of the SEC budget they have  more than enough resources to do a  better job  of policing the mutual fund industry  particularly with respect to hidden fees  compensation of managers and a whole  host of other areas I just don't think  the establishment of another SRO is the  way to go  yes with the demise of Arthur Andersen  could you tell us what the changes are  that are taking place in the accounting  profession and whether it is adequate  for in the long term I believe that the  government's case against Arthur  Andersen was very unfortunate it was  unfortunate because Arthur Andersen  under paul volcker at that point had  committed to embrace a series of very  very constructive changes that would  have served as a model for the  profession as a whole when anderson  failed not only did thousands of people  who had nothing to do with the Enron  episode lose their jobs but competition  was reduced and the remaining big four  was much less willing to embrace the  sorts of changes that Andersen was going  to embrace as a result of Anderson's  demise and the Enron debacle Congress  came up with the proposal to create an  accounting oversight board made up of  individuals of integrity who understood  the issues and could take over the  responsibility here 24 performed by the  aicpa which is the cheerleading group  the trade group for the accounting  profession which has done a perfectly  miserable job of representing the  interests of America's accountants this  group would conduct peer reviews to see  to it that audits were accomplished  professionally and they would revise  review and recreate audit standards  which heretofore had been zealously  protected by the AICPA  now I think this group can be effective  it can be effective because the  accounting industry abdicated their  right to self regulation and called for  oversight now if this oversight is fair  and effective I think over a period of  time the industry could be given back  its right to self regulation the key to  all of this I believe is the kind of  person that bill Donaldson the next  chairman of the SEC will choose to head  that oversight body the person in my  judgment should be someone of such  integrity and probity that he or she but  broadly be recognized by the American  public as someone who really had their  interests at heart I think that the  profession itself under the  sarbanes-oxley Act has begun the process  of divested itself of much of their  consulting work much of their tax work  and many of the conflicts that afflicted  them I have a piece that will run  shortly in the wall street journal on  exactly those areas of sarbanes-oxley  that I think represent real investor  protection and real benefits yes we've  talked about and the American public  they're concerned about the pay-to-play  concept with them analysts on Wall  Street and then there's a conflict of  interest between audits and consulting  some argue that there is a conflict of  interest in terms of pay to play with  industry analyst and tech firms and  other types of companies in terms of you  by research from certain analyst firms  and they'll start to write up and talk  about your products and everything could  you shed light on  where you see us addressing this type of  conflict and it is there as a conflict  from your perspective yeah I think there  is a conflict and as I think back of my  days the sec I could have done more on  this issue that I did and I understood  the conflict at that time having headed  a brokerage firm in a stock exchange but  I was so obsessed with other issues that  I relied on the NASD and the New York  Stock Exchange to address the issue and  only when it became a national scandal  was action taken on this issue but like  anybody else analysts became very  closely allied to companies that they  covered which were willing to give them  additional information the economics of  the brokerage business changed after  fixed Commission's were abandoned and  now no longer was retail brokerage the  principal financial driver behind most  brokerage firms and corporate finance  became the most important source of  revenue and analysts became adjuncts of  the corporate finance department rather  than of the retail sales department  analysts went out and made pitches i  presently serve on the boards of several  private companies one of which was  seeking a public offering and of the  five firms that made presentations to us  five of those presentations were made by  analysts not by people in the corporate  finance department and that's wrong now  what do we do about it well  the Attorney General of the state of New  York who has been criticized broadly I  think did something that was very  constructive and very creative and very  innovative the SEC was in the midst of a  huge political controversy and clearly  distracted from a number of issues he  took advantage of a law in the state of  New York that enabled him to bring an  action against firms for literally for  making fraudulent use of their research  analysts and that resulted in a  settlement where the firm's made certain  commitments to split off research from  Investment Banking to see to it that  investment banking didn't pay the  analysts and so forth I think as far as  it went that was constructive but the  millions of dollars in penalties paid by  the firms in my judgment was trivial was  meaningless but the only real benefit of  such an action comes from the public  perception of wrongdoing that went on in  this industry we don't know yet how all  of this is going to play out the  settlement still has not called for an  admission on the part of the firms as to  what they did I doubt that that  admission will go into every detail of  what they did and that would be the real  value of a settlement a true confession  of abuse a true confession of wrongdoing  if you move the research department as a  number of firms have done from the 31st  floor to the 16th floor and give it  another boss you know it may diminish  the conflict if it's in the same firm  under the same roof with the  same economics the perceptual conflict  of is there and I think that's wrong and  I think the only response is a total  separation of research and investment  banking and that is not likely to happen  what is likely to happen I believe is  the growth of a number of specialized  research firms that have already grown  up and can charge fees for quality  research which would be viewed with the  kind of attention that ratings by rating  agencies receive today it will be kind  of a stamp of approval so I think the  situation is better it is far from being  cured and sell-side research still  enjoys a fairly dim reputation I'll try  to give shorter answers what is your  opinion about Senator Grassley's recent  letter to President Bush and some  members of the SEC about disclosing tax  returns to investors do you think that  would help them or make them more  confused you know I didn't hear that  could you repeat that what is your  opinion about Senator Grassley's recent  letter to President Bush and some  members of the SEC about disclosing tax  returns or tax return information to  investors do you think that would help  investors or just make them more  confused I can't think of any kind of  disclosure any kind of disclosure that i  think would not be useful for investors  our markets are based on full and fair  disclosure not merit regulation and the  traditional business argument against  disclosure is it would confuse investors  we don't want to show the portfolio's of  mutual funds more than once a year that  would confuse investors we don't want to  reveal the proxy votes of mutual funds  that would burden investors I don't buy  that so I'm supportive of  a call for greater disclosure crisis and  confidence is multifaceted how would you  align the resources of the SEC and set  the priorities of the SEC if you were in  Bill Donaldson shoes read my column in  The Wall Street Journal but I think  first of all technology has to be used  more effectively by the Commission the  fact that when the World Trade Center  blew up the Commission lost all of the  records of all of the cases in its  second most important office and simply  didn't have the technology to replicate  those records the fact that the division  of corporation finance is so back backed  up that they can review filings only  once every five years which is  tantamount to no review at all we need  technology to address that issue the  Commission needs additional trial  attorneys to bring cases they need  technology to address internet fraud  they need many more accountants and the  market for accounts today for those of  you that are studying accounting you  have entered the Garden of Eden you  accountants are more sought after today  than just about anyone else in the  business community and the Commission  needs them badly and unfortunately can't  pay them as much as they can get in the  private sector the Commission needs more  funds for investor education as I said  before a dollar spent in educating  investors is far more useful than a  dollar spent in regulating our markets I  think the issue of deciding what  accounting standards are going to be and  how will the Commission prevent  additional accounting fraud is terribly  important  I think perhaps the most important  mission for the Commission is to revise  the way America's accounting standards  are set we've had the independent  standard setter the fasb setting  accounting standards but being funded by  the very organizations the very business  groups for whom they're setting  standards that conflict has created  enormous bureaucracy and the way  accounting standards have been set and  now that Congress has provided the  funding for an independent fasb i hope  the Commission can work with the fasb to  see to it that their process of standard  setting becomes speedier more relevant  more understandable and most importantly  we should be moving toward international  accounting standards Paul Volcker and  the International Accounting Standards  Board are doing a darn good job in  setting accounting standards  particularly with respect to the  expensing of stock options I hope the  fasb follow suits and I hope that we are  able to harmonize accounting standards  using the best of us standards and some  of the best standards that are out there  in the past we could always assume if  its US GAAP it's got to be right well  Enron Anderson changed a lot of things  and I don't think we can afford to say  just because it's here just because it's  gap it's right we still have the best  markets of any in the world the best  standards the best process but we still  can learn a lot and make adjustments in  where we are now you ask me about my  relationship with President Clinton  and that's an interesting question i had  at the commission as my predecessors and  successors add about 25 political  appointees now political appointees are  those that traditionally come from the  party in power and are cleared by the  White House personnel office at the  Commission some 16 or 17 of the people  that I appointed to those offices were  either independence or Republicans and  the Clinton White House did not object  to a single one of them never sent me  any one that had been active in a  campaign to employ and greatly respected  the independence of the agency at a time  when our funding was jeopardized I went  to Larry Summers and told him that our  bill is combined with a lot of important  bills and I know the pressure on  President Clinton to sign that bill will  be enormous but I hope you can get him  to agree to veto the bill if the  viability of the SEC is placed in  question within several hours Larry came  back and said the president has agreed  to do it he was enormously supportive I  met with him alone just about the only  time that I did meet with him alone a  day or so before he left office and  after the usual kind of formalities I  said mr. president you know I wondered  why I haven't heard from you more or  seen more of you during eight years I've  been here we've certainly been involved  in a lot of issues some of them were  pretty tough for you in terms of  the politics of the issue and I just  wondered if you felt about me as you  might a postmaster in Little Rock any  smile then he said no he said with you  and Bob Rubin Larry Summers and some of  the others that I knew knew one another  in private life and were real  professionals I respected you and I  wanted to allow you to do your job and I  think that intelligence and that  sensitivity produced a darn good  economic team and the fact that Reuben  and I and Greenspan had known one  another for many years really helped  policy-making when you get to Washington  your staff is inclined to tell you well  you can't trust Treasury or you can't  trust the controllers office they'll eat  your lunch and they'll play on the turf  battles well they couldn't do that with  us because we knew one another too well  and I think that element of trust really  helped America's markets so thank you  for that very perceptive question  anything else yes I have a question  about investor protection and the  relationship between the unsophisticated  investor and a broker my question is  whether in your view full disclosure is  adequately met by a broker sending a  prospectus with a trade confirmation and  if that's not adequate what what is the  industry standard or if it's if the  industry standard is has some  shortcomings what should it be well I  having been a broker having seen the  conflicts that brokers and firms go  through i feel that most investors  should if they have a broker that's  charging commissions should fire that  broker which is the heading of chapter  one but i think we are leaving the era  of the commission broker and entering an  era of financial advisor i think most  investors would do well most investors  in the first place should in my judgment  be in low expense ratio mutual funds  index funds that mirror the performance  of the market as a whole but an investor  who has substantially larger amounts of  money should seek an investment advisor  or a broker who charges an annual fee as  far as the protections are concerned  there's a lot that doesn't come across  in the prospectus or in the confirmation  like  how much if it's a principal transaction  a security that a bond that has been  inventoried by the firm what their  profit might be like whether the broker  is being paid a commission and if so how  much what market was the order executed  on there's a great many things that is  there a contest that the broker is  participating in where his Commission's  might send him to Honolulu so I think  disclosure can go a lot further and I  think the conflicts that the firms have  are beginning to diminish some firms are  doing better than others and I think  that the real quality to look for in a  broker is how smart is that broker how  well does he know the products that he's  selling so many new products are  available today yes what are your  thoughts on the relationship between the  Bush administration and the SEC the  relationship between the Bush  administration and the SEC I think the  president has gone along with a  substantial increase in the budget of  the SEC he has spoken about the  importance of stamping out fraud in our  markets has put the prestige of the  administration behind the Commission and  I think you can ask for no more from an  administration and I applaud the  initiatives that he has embraced  yes my ibanking colleagues are gonna  dislike this question flawed in the  sense that prices are set deliberately  low money's left on the table from a for  the company that's going public and  retail investors are generally shut out  what is your opinion on that and do you  think government and the SEC  specifically has a role in helping to  change that and make it more efficient  I'm often asked this question about the  fact that I POS initial public offerings  wind up in the hands of institutions and  very little of it goes to retail  investors well I don't think the process  of allocating I POS the basic process of  allocating it to your best customer is  wrong I think that's a standard and a  reasonable business practice I believe  that if there is if there are payments  involve for those allocations if  investors are expected to buy in the  aftermarket or buy at higher prices  that's a manipulative practice and  that's a practice that is a violation of  the securities laws and those instances  have been under investigation and I  think you will see cases brought by the  Commission as they should be I think  that the practice of using research to  hype companies that really are  undeserving of that is wrong and I think  American investors have to be far more  questioning about buying IPOs I think  anyone who makes the purchase of IPOs  the basis of their investing strategy  deserves to lose their money it's a dumb  strategy most IPOs don't work out  and it's the greed factor at work and I  don't have much sympathy for investors  who play that game and then complain  because they find that they've lost  money but to the extent to which there  have been manipulations that have taken  place that is that is totally wrong  hi exactly a question that I have is you  mentioned several times about investor  education and I know your book is one of  the ways to educate and University of  Michigan could you be a little bit more  specific as to what other area that we  could become a better private investor  well I can't think of a better area than  investor education I think that with  respect to 401ks that's the greatest  universe of uneducated investors in the  world the Labor Department simply lacks  the resources to do what they should do  to teach investors on which options  should be pursued and how to understand  when a proxy is really reveals a  terrible flaw in a company and I think  that process of education is not  something that can necessarily be taught  in high schools or universities or  graduate schools it's pretty pretty  obvious pretty plain and i think that  the SEC has begun that process and  should continue it I think the town  meetings allowing investors to ask  questions is a pretty good way of doing  it I think that investor protection in  this country is a function of kind of a  trilogy of three things the enforcement  efforts of the SEC to bring actions  against violations of our security laws  they can't assure investors against all  fraud but they can see the most common  cases internet fraud for instance and  they can come down hard on those cases  making them a symbol for what investors  should avoid secondly  the work of the self-regulating  organizations the work of the New York  Stock Exchange and the NASD whose  enforcement arm is has become  increasingly sophisticated to protect  investors the NASD is about to bring a  major case involving research on its  misuse and finally private rights of  action the rights of individuals to sue  under our securities laws when they have  been defrauded and those three elements  private rights of action SROs and the  enforcement efforts of the SEC in my  judgment give us the best protected  universe of investors and the best  protected markets in the history of the  world yes  I have any of the problems you've talked  about tonight been increased or enabled  by the Securities Reform Act of nineteen  ninety five and have any of the  provisions of that been repealed the  Securities Reform Act that of 1995 that  I was just asked about the question was  whether any of the problems resulted  from that act which to some extent made  it somewhat more difficult to bring  frivolous lawsuits I don't really  believe so I think there were some  aspects of that act that were productive  I think that the SEC tried to see to it  that investor interests were protected  and I think the greatest fears that we  had were not realized the act as it was  originally constructed called for losers  to pay for litigation and I oppose that  strenuously because I felt that would  simply say that people who didn't have  money simply couldn't afford to sue and  I think that's a an essential protection  and the Act didn't include that I do not  personally believe the litigation reform  act brought about what we have  experienced in recent months and years I  think what brought about what we've  experienced in recent months and years  has been the failure of all our  gatekeepers and what I referred you  before as the suspension of disbelief by  a public that was willing to go along  with anything by research analysts who  saw every recommendation go up by  investment bankers who saw every  underwriting succeed by brokers or so  recommendation go up in all of these  energies thought it was their genius  that produce these results rather than  the sweep of a bull market which carried  a lot of bad stuff along with a lot of  good  well you've been a great audience I  won't ask you any additional questions I  hope that some of you buy my book but  more importantly than that I hope you  kind of carry the message ask the  questions find out whether you're your  Congressman your senator cares read  proxy statements look for boards that  are corrupted we've got lots of them  because you know the American people are  a friendly people we're a collegial  people and when we're invited to go on  boards we tend to go if we know people  on those boards or ave serve on our  boards and how likely are we to raise  our hands and object well before Enron  Anderson not very likely today I think  audit committees and boards of directors  have been put on notice and I'm very  often challenged with well it's going to  be so hard to get directors today it  will and it should be because directors  have a great and almost sacred  responsibility and they're basically the  lifeline in terms of investor protection  that will do much more than any of the  rules or congressional fulminations or  anything we write about or speak about  and some of you serve on boards will  serve on boards no board members and  I hope you won't despair you'll ask the  tough questions and you'll keep coming  back thanks a lot  Arthur Levitt served as chairman of the  SEC during the Clinton administration  from nineteen ninety three to two  thousand his book is take on the street  what Wall Street in corporate America  don't want you to know what you can do  to fight back it's published by Pantheon  books an imprint of Random House visit  them online at Random House calm /  pantheon monday is President's Day and  you're watching a three-day weekend of  books on book
 

